This is a geometry problem.  We can use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the area of the square.  The Pythagorean Theorem states that the sum of the squares of the lengths of the legs of a right triangle is equal to the square of the length of the hypotenuse.  In this case, the legs of the right triangle are the sides of the square, and the hypotenuse is the distance from the center of the ellipse to the center of the square.  We can find the distance from the center of the ellipse to the center of the square by finding the distance from the center of the ellipse to the center of the square, and then subtracting the radius of the ellipse.  The distance from the center of the ellipse to the center of the square is \sqrt{3^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{45} = 3\sqrt{5}.  The radius of the ellipse is 1.  Therefore, the distance from the center of the ellipse to the center of the square is 3\sqrt{5} - 1 = 3\sqrt{5} - 1.  The area of the square is the square of the length of the hypotenuse, which is (3\sqrt{5} - 1)^2 = 45 - 6\sqrt{5} + 1 = 46 - 6\sqrt{5}.  Therefore, the area of the square is \boxed{46 - 6\sqrt{5}}.

